C = [
("h1", "Preface"),
("p", "This volume argues a position. It does not gather what churches say about Elijah and leave the reader to choose. It says what the author believes the Scriptures teach about the prophet who was promised before the day of the LORD, it gives the evidence from the verses in the order the verses stand, and it answers the strongest objections in the objectors’ own words, so that a reader who disagrees knows exactly where."),
("p", "The question is narrow, and the prophet who closes the Old Testament raises it. The last words of Malachi are a promise and a warning: “Behold, I will send you Elijah the prophet before the coming of the great and dreadful day of the LORD: and he shall turn the heart of the fathers to the children, and the heart of the children to their fathers, lest I come and smite the earth with a curse” (Malachi 4:5, 6). The Old Testament ends on that verse, and when the Gospels begin, the promise is on everyone’s lips. Priests and Levites ask John, “Art thou Elias?” (John 1:21). The disciples ask the Lord, “Why then say the scribes that Elias must first come?” (Matthew 17:10). The crowd at Caesarea Philippi says that some think Jesus himself is Elias (Matthew 16:14). At the cross, someone hears the cry and says, “Let be, let us see whether Elias will come to save him” (Matthew 27:49)."),
("p", "Readers have answered the question in three ways. Some say the promise was kept in John the Baptist and is finished. Some say it was not kept at all, and that John had nothing to do with it. Some say it was kept in part and still waits. Each reading can quote a verse. The first quotes “Elias is come already” (Matthew 17:12), the second quotes John’s “I am not” (John 1:21), and the third quotes “Elias truly shall first come, and restore all things” (Matthew 17:11). This volume holds that all three verses stand, that they were spoken by different persons to different hearers for different ends, and that none of them is allowed to cancel the others."),
("p", "The position is this. Malachi promises one Elijah the prophet, sent by the LORD, before the great and dreadful day. The Gospels answer in three voices. Gabriel tells Zacharias that his son will go before the Lord “in the spirit and power of Elias” (Luke 1:17). John, asked to his face whether he is Elias, says, “I am not” (John 1:21). The Lord says of John, “And if ye will receive it, this is Elias, which was for to come” (Matthew 11:14), and then, after John is dead, says both that “Elias truly shall first come, and restore all things” and that “Elias is come already, and they knew him not” (Matthew 17:11, 12). The volume reads the three voices together as an offer made to Israel in John’s day and conditional on its reception. The offer was refused, the restoring of all things is not reported done, the day of the LORD has not come, and the text therefore points to a further coming of Elijah before that day."),
("p", "Three things about the method. First, the volume reads the King James Version only, and every Scripture quotation is checked word for word against it. Second, each chapter opens with a claim, reads its passages in order, and closes with a short table and a list of what the volume takes from the passages, so that the reader can see what has been argued and test it. Third, where modern writers are named, they are named for a position the author has read in a free text, and where an objection is stated, it is stated as the objector would state it and then answered from the verses."),
("p", "The volume does not take up what its neighbours argue. Prophetic Commentary Volume 2 sets Malachi 4:5, 6 and Matthew 17:10, 11 beside the false prophet of Revelation 13 as the counterfeit of Elijah; this volume does not re-argue that counterfeit and asks only who the genuine prophet is. The two witnesses of Revelation 11 are read in the places that read them; this volume sets the chapter beside Malachi, lists what the text of Revelation 11 itself says, and does not put a name on the witnesses. The Lord’s ministry to Israel is the subject of Gospel Commentary Volume 2, the apostles’ question in Acts 1:6 and the times of restitution in Acts 3:21 of Acts Commentary Volume 1, and the day of the LORD of Study 7. The volume cites these and does not repeat them. It also leaves aside what it cannot settle from the text: the date of the day, the place and the hour of Elijah’s coming, and the question of what he will say."),
("p", "One boundary runs through the whole volume. Every passage here is addressed to Israel. Malachi speaks to Israel by the hand of a prophet (Malachi 1:1), Gabriel to a priest of Israel in the temple, John to the people at the Jordan, and the Lord to his disciples about Israel’s expectation. The Body of Christ is not named in any of these passages and is not read into them. The volume draws nothing from them for the Body’s calling, hope or conduct."),
("h1", "Thesis"),
("p", "Malachi promises one Elijah the prophet before the day of the LORD. John came in the spirit and power of Elias and was offered to Israel as the Elias who was to come; Israel did not receive him, and the Lord, speaking after John’s death, still says that Elias shall first come and restore all things."),
("ctbl", "The thesis in four statements.", [
    ("Statement", "What it says", "Where it is argued"),
    ("1", "The promise is of one man, Elijah the prophet, sent by the LORD before the great and dreadful day. The messenger of Malachi 3:1 and Isaiah 40:3 is a second promise, and the Gospels join the two on John.", "Chapters 1, 4 and 5"),
    ("2", "Gabriel, John and the Lord each speak of John’s relation to Elijah in different words, and each statement stands as written: in the spirit and power of Elias; “I am not”; “if ye will receive it, this is Elias.”", "Chapters 6, 7 and 8"),
    ("3", "The Lord’s answer on the mountain has two parts that the volume does not let cancel each other: “Elias truly shall first come, and restore all things,” and “Elias is come already, and they knew him not.” John is dead when the first is spoken.", "Chapters 9 and 11"),
    ("4", "The restoring of all things is not reported done, the day of the LORD has not come, and the text therefore holds a further coming of Elijah before that day, in Israel’s line; the Body of Christ is not in these passages.", "Chapters 10 and 11"),
  ], [0.17, 0.57, 0.26]),
("p", "A reader may test the thesis in one move. Put the three statements of John’s relation to Elijah side by side, and ask whether the text lets any one of them be dropped. If Gabriel’s sentence is dropped, John’s “I am not” and the Lord’s “this is Elias” stand against each other with nothing to join them. If John’s denial is dropped, the Lord’s words are heard as simple identity and the question of the priests and Levites is left without an answer. If the Lord’s second sentence on the mountain is dropped, he said only that Elias had come, and Matthew 17:11 has nothing to say. If the first is dropped, the Lord’s own “truly shall first come” has nothing to mean. The thesis stands if the four stand together; the volume argues that they do."),
]
